At 720x450, fps 18 (vs 8 at 1440x900) — scaling with pixel count confirms software rasterization of the canvas is the bottleneck in this environment. On real hardware with GPU canvas, this will be fine. But to be safe for low-end devices, I should optimize draw cost:

- Larger glow sprites scaled up are expensive fill (overdraw). Motes were removed during gameplay, good.
- Full-screen background gradient each frame — expensive fill but unavoidable; can pre-render background gradient/grid/stars to an offscreen canvas and blit? Background is mostly static except stars drifting and grid drift. Could pre-render gradient to a 1x1? Actually fillRect with gradient covers 1.3MP each frame → software raster ~costly. Options:
  - Bake the base gradient + grid LINE layers into offscreen canvas once per resize, then drawImage each frame (fast blit).
  - Stars: 190 fillRects, negligible.
  - But blitting a full-screen image is also 1.3MP copy, similar to gradient fill but cheaper than gradient evaluation.
  - Keep dynamic elements.

Given real devices have GPU-accelerated canvas, 18fps at 720p software is not representative. But mobile software? Modern phones have GPU.

Still, the main FPS measurement at 1440x900 in this environment would make screenshot verification of animation less meaningful, but static screenshots fine.

Can we enable GPU in this Playwright config? args use vulkan swiftshader, which is CPU. The AGENTS.md notes "Report SwiftShader as CPU software rendering." So environment intentionally CPU. So 7-10fps at 1440x900 is expected here; not necessarily a bug.

