content.text:
{
  "scrollH": 390,
  "clientH": 390,
  "overflow": 844
}

Landscape menu has a problem: the tiles row is cut off at the bottom (only top of tiles visible), and the hero occupies left with tiles below (because <=860 breakpoint makes grid 1 column). At 844x390 landscape, we'd rather have the desktop 2-column layout to fit height. The media query `(max-width: 860px)` triggers at 844, stacking. Then the `(max-height: 620px) and (orientation: landscape)` rules shrink some but the grid is still stacked, causing overflow (scrollH 390 though? It says scrollH=390 equal; tiles are cut because .menu has overflow auto? It reported scrollH 390 = clientH, so content may be overflowing invisibly clipped? The screenshot shows tiles partially cut at 390 height. hmm).

Better: for landscape phones, use the 2-column layout. Change breakpoints:
- Base 2-column.
- Switch to 1-column only when narrow AND portrait-ish. Use `@media (max-width: 860px) and (orientation: portrait)`? But tablets 768x1024 portrait → 1 col, fine. Phones 844x390 landscape → keep 2 cols. Desktop small windows (800x600) would keep 2 cols (base) which might be cramped but okay.

Alternatively add a landscape-specific override to restore 2 columns:
```css
@media (orientation: landscape) and (max-height: 620px) {
  .menu-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(280px,0.9fr); gap: 24px; }
  .menu-tiles { flex-direction: column; }
  .menu-tiles .tile { flex: initial; }
  ...
}
```
But the <=860 rules set `.menu-tiles { flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap }` and tile flex 1 1 220. In landscape 844, we'd want tiles in a column on the right.

Simplest: change the stacking breakpoint to 860px + portrait, so landscape 844 stays 2-col. Also landscape 667x375 (iPhone SE landscape) would use base 2-col — might be cramped: hero 1.35fr + tiles 0.8fr with gap 72 (5vw) at 667 → hero 356, tiles 211; tiles min 300 from base grid-template `minmax(300px, 0.8fr)` → total 356+300+33=689 >667 → overflow. Hmm.

Use a dedicated landscape rule for small heights with 2 columns and smaller gaps/fonts:
```css
@media (orientation: landscape) and (max-height: 620px) {
  .menu { padding: 16px 22px; }
  .menu-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(240px, 0.85fr); gap: 26px; }
  .menu-tiles { flex-direction: column; flex-wrap: nowrap; gap: 8px; }
  .menu-tiles .tile { flex: 0 0 auto; padding: 10px 12px; }
  .eyebrow, .tagline { display: none; }
  .hero-title h1 { font-size: clamp(40px, 7vw, 62px); }
  .hero-orb { width: 52px; }
  .hero-actions { margin-top: 14px; }
  .hero-foot { margin-top: 14px; }
  .btn-lg { padding: 12px 20px; }
  .menu-footer { display: none; }
  .tile-icon { width: 32px; height: 32px; }
  .tile-copy small { display: none; } /* compact */
  .tile-rank { display: none; }
  .menu-grid { align-content: center; }
  .menu { justify-content: center; }
}
```
The existing landscape query is `@media (max-height: 620px) and (orientation: landscape)` with a few rules; replace/expand it. But the <=860 query (earlier in cascade) sets `.menu-tiles` to row and `.tile` flex 1 1 220; the landscape query comes later so it can override.

Order in CSS: 860 block, 560 block, 384 block, 680 block, 620 landscape block, reduced motion. So the landscape block is last and wins. Good.

At 844x390, landscape block sets 2 columns → fits.

At 667x375, 2 columns with min 240: 667-44 padding=623; gap 26; hero gets 623-26-240=357; fine.

At 568x320 landscape (small): grid 2 cols: 568-44=524; gap26; tiles min240; hero 258; h1 clamp(40,7vw=40,62)=40; "GREEN" width ~140; fine. Height 320: hero content h1 2 lines (80) + actions 45 + foot 20 = 145; fits.

Let me rewrite the landscape query.

Also the current screenshot shows the tiles partially cut; after fix it will be side-by-side.

Implement.

